## Reflection

<details style="margin-top: 12px; border-radius: 8px; overflow: hidden;">
  <summary style="background-color: #E4E2F8; padding: 12px 16px; cursor: pointer; font-weight: bold; color: #1F1976; font-size: 16px;">
    &#128161; Across Lessons 5-7, what's the one thread connecting slope and y-intercept, loss, and biased data?
  </summary>
  <div style="background-color: #FFFFFF; padding: 16px; border: 1px solid #ACA8EA; font-size: 16px; color: #1A1A1A; line-height: 1.6;">
    Every one of these is a different way of asking "how good is this model, really?" Slope and y-intercept describe what the model says. Loss measures how wrong it is. Bias asks whether its training data even matches the situation it's being used in. A model can be well-built and still fail, if the data behind it was never representative in the first place.
  </div>
</details>

